[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a quick‑reference guide that shows **all the practical ways you can add a search / geocoder box to a Mapbox GL JS v2 map**, together with the code you need, the licensing / cost implications, and the trade‑offs you’ll face when you choose one.

---

## 1. The “official” Mapbox option – **Mapbox GL Geocoder**

| Feature | Details |
|---------|---------|
| **Package** | `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder` (npm) or the CDN version `https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v4.7.2/mapbox-gl-geocoder.min.js` |
| **License** | BSD‑3 (open source) – but it **requires a Mapbox access token** and is subject to Mapbox’s usage limits & pricing. |
| **How it works** | The control sends requests to the **Mapbox Geocoding API** (`https://api.mapbox.com/geocoding/v5/...`). The API returns a list of candidates, the control renders a dropdown, and you can listen to the `result` event to get the selected feature. |
| **Pros** | • Plug‑and‑play – just a few lines of code.<br>• Fully integrated UI (autocomplete, keyboard navigation, clear button).<br>• Handles forward & reverse geocoding, bounding‑box biasing, language, proximity, etc.<br>• Works with Mapbox GL v2 out‑of‑the‑box. |
| **Cons** | • You must keep a **Mapbox access token** (costs per 1 000 requests after the free tier).<br>• Tied to Mapbox’s terms of service (no self‑hosting of the geocoder).<br>• If you ever migrate to MapLibre you’ll need a different plugin. |
| **Installation** | ```bash\nnpm i @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder\n```\nor via CDN (add after the Mapbox GL script). |
| **Basic usage** | ```js\nimport mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';\nimport MapboxGeocoder from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder';\nimport '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css';\n\nmapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN';\nconst map = new mapboxgl.Map({\n  container: 'map',\n  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',\n  center: [-122.44, 37.76],\n  zoom: 12\n});\n\nconst geocoder = new MapboxGeocoder({\n  accessToken: mapboxgl.accessToken,\n  mapboxgl: mapboxgl,          // required for the built‑in marker\n  placeholder: 'Search places…',\n  proximity: { longitude: -122.44, latitude: 37.76 }, // optional bias\n});\n\nmap.addControl(geocoder);\n\ngeocoder.on('result', (e) => {\n  console.log('selected', e.result);\n});\n``` |
| **Customization** | • `marker: false` to suppress the default marker.<br>• `flyTo: { speed: 1.2 }` to control animation.<br>• `countries`, `bbox`, `language`, `types`, `autocomplete` etc. are all supported. |
| **Rate limits** | 100 000 free requests / month (as of 2024). After that, $0.75 per 1 000 forward‑geocode requests. See the Mapbox pricing page for the latest numbers. |

---

## 2. Open‑source / self‑hosted alternatives (no Mapbox token)

If you want to avoid Mapbox’s pricing or you’re already planning to move to **MapLibre**, you can swap the geocoder backend while keeping (or lightly adapting) the UI. Below are the most common combos.

| Option | Backend (Geocoding service) | UI library (control) | License | Typical cost |
|--------|----------------------------|----------------------|---------|--------------|
| **MapLibre‑GL‑Geocoder** (fork of Mapbox‑GL‑Geocoder) | Any HTTP‑compatible geocoder (Nominatim, Photon, Pelias, MapTiler, etc.) | `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` (npm) | BSD‑3 | Free if you self‑host the backend; otherwise pay the provider. |
| **Algolia Places** (now deprecated, still works) | Algolia’s hosted index of OSM data | `places.js` + custom control | MIT | Free tier (5 000 req/day) then paid. |
| **Leaflet‑Control‑Geocoder** (adapted for Mapbox GL) | Nominatim, Photon, Pelias, MapTiler, etc. | Custom wrapper (you embed a Leaflet control inside a Mapbox GL container) | MIT | Free (self‑host) |
| **Mapbox Search SDK (Web)** | Mapbox Search API (newer, token‑based) | `@mapbox/search-js` UI components | BSD‑3 | Same pricing as Mapbox Geocoder (but more modern features). |
| **Custom UI + fetch** | Any REST geocoder (Nominatim, Photon, Pelias, MapTiler, OpenCage) | Build your own dropdown / autocomplete | — | Depends on provider (often free for low volume). |

Below we dive into the two most “drop‑in” replacements: **MapLibre‑GL‑Geocoder** (the easiest if you’re already using Mapbox‑GL‑Geocoder) and a **pure‑HTML/JS custom UI** that talks to a public Nominatim endpoint.

---

### 2A. MapLibre‑GL‑Geocoder (drop‑in replacement)

**Why you’d pick this:**  
- Same API surface as `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`.  
- Works with Mapbox GL v2 **and** MapLibre GL v6 (just import the right namespace).  
- You can point it at any geocoder that follows the **Mapbox Geocoding API response shape** (or you can provide a small adapter).

#### Installation

```bash
npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder
# optional UI CSS (same as Mapbox’s)
npm i @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder   # just for the CSS, or copy from CDN
```

#### Minimal example (using **Nominatim** as the backend)

```js
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';               // still using Mapbox GL v2
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';
import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css';

// Mapbox GL still needs a token for the map style; the geocoder does NOT.
mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN';

const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
  center: [-122.44, 37.76],
  zoom: 12
});

// Create a geocoder that talks to Nominatim
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // No accessToken needed for the geocoder itself
  placeholder: 'Search OSM places…',
  // The `forwardGeocode` function is the only required hook.
  // It receives the user query and must return a Promise that resolves
  // to an object with a `features` array (Mapbox‑style GeoJSON).
  forwardGeocode: async (query) => {
    const url = new URL('https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search');
    url.searchParams.set('q', query);
    url.searchParams.set('format', 'geojson');
    url.searchParams.set('addressdetails', '1');
    url.searchParams.set('limit', '5');

    const response = await fetch(url);
    const data = await response.json();

    // Nominatim already returns GeoJSON FeatureCollection,
    // but we need to rename the property to `features`.
    return {
      features: data.features.map(f => ({
        // Keep the original geometry
        geometry: f.geometry,
        // The `place_name` field is what the UI shows
        place_name: f.properties.display_name,
        // `center` is required for the map to jump to the result
        center: f.geometry.coordinates,
        // Keep any other properties you like
        properties: f.properties,
        // Give each result a unique id (optional)
        id: f.properties.osm_id
      }))
    };
  },
  // Optional: you can also implement `reverseGeocode` if you need it.
  // reverseGeocode: async (result) => { … }
});

map.addControl(geocoder);
```

**Key points**

- **No token** is needed for the geocoder itself; you only keep the Mapbox token for the map style.
- The **`forwardGeocode`** hook must return a **Mapbox‑style** response (`{features: [...]}`) – you can adapt any service to that shape.
- If you later switch to a different backend (MapTiler, Pelias, etc.) you only change the `forwardGeocode` implementation.
- The UI (autocomplete dropdown, keyboard navigation, clear button) is **identical** to the Mapbox plugin, because the codebase is a fork.

#### Using a hosted service (MapTiler, OpenCage, etc.)

If you prefer a hosted API that already returns Mapbox‑compatible JSON, you can skip the adapter:

```js
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // MapTiler Geocoding API (requires a MapTiler key)
  accessToken: 'YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY',   // <-- note: this is NOT a Mapbox token
  // The library will automatically call https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding/{query}.json
});
```

Check the provider’s docs for the exact query‑parameter names; most of them follow the Mapbox schema.

---

### 2B. Build a tiny custom UI that talks to **Nominatim** (or any other public geocoder)

If you want **full control over the look‑and‑feel** or you’re already using a UI framework (React, Vue, Svelte, etc.), you can skip the pre‑built control entirely.

#### Example (plain HTML + vanilla JS)

```html
<div id="search-container" style="position:absolute;top:10px;left:10px;z-index:1;">
  <input id="search-input" type="text" placeholder="Search OSM…" style="width:250px;padding:4px;">
  <ul id="search-results" style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;background:#fff;border:1px solid #ccc;max-height:200px;overflow:auto;display:none;"></ul>
</div>
<div id="map" style="width:100%;height:100vh;"></div>

<script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.js"></script>
<link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
<script>
  mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN';
  const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
    center: [-122.44, 37.76],
    zoom: 12
  });

  const input = document.getElementById('search-input');
  const results = document.getElementById('search-results');

  let debounceTimer;
  input.addEventListener('input', (e) => {
    clearTimeout(debounceTimer);
    const query = e.target.value.trim();
    if (!query) { results.style.display = 'none'; return; }

    debounceTimer = setTimeout(() => {
      fetch(`https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=geojson&q=${encodeURIComponent(query)}&limit=5`)
        .then(r => r.json())
        .then(data => {
          results.innerHTML = '';
          data.features.forEach(f => {
            const li = document.createElement('li');
            li.textContent = f.properties.display_name;
            li.style.padding = '4px';
            li.style.cursor = 'pointer';
            li.addEventListener('click', () => {
              const [lng, lat] = f.geometry.coordinates;
              map.flyTo({center: [lng, lat], zoom: 14});
              results.style.display = 'none';
            });
            results.appendChild(li);
          });
          results.style.display = 'block';
        });
    }, 300); // simple debounce
  });

  // Hide results when clicking outside
  document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    if (!searchContainer.contains(e.target)) results.style.display = 'none';
